<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sfatul Țării</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Council of the Country</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sfatul Țării, the 'Council of the Country', was the regional assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> formed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a> and meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chisinau">Chișinău</a>. On 2 December 1917 it proclaimed the Moldavian Democratic Republic. With Romanian troops in the country, it voted on 9 April 1918 (27 March Old Style) for union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and dissolved itself later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sfatul-tarii.jpg" alt="Sfatul Țării" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alex:D · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Sfatul_Tarii.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a>, teachers and intellectuals set up committees in almost every village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, and congresses of peasants, teachers, cooperators and clergy followed. In April 1917 the First Congress of Cooperatives of Bessarabia, meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chisinau">Chișinău</a>, demanded autonomy and a legislative assembly called Sfatul Țării. The National Moldavian Party, formed the same month under the boyar Vasile Stroescu, pressed for autonomy too, while Moldavian soldiers organised a Central Soldiers' Committee for All of Bessarabia. When the Ukrainian Central Rada claimed the province, the Commissar Constantin Mimi gathered delegates to reject the claim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In November 1917 the soldiers' council proclaimed the autonomy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> and called elections. The soldiers' councils chose 44 deputies, the Peasants' Congress 36, and county and communal commissions with professional and ethnic associations the remaining 70. The diet had first been planned with 120 seats, 70 per cent for Moldavians to match estimates of the population, and was enlarged to 135 and then 150. About 70 per cent of its members were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and the rest Russians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-inculet">Ion Inculeț</a>, sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kerensky">Alexander Kerensky</a>, became president of the republic it proclaimed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sfatul Țării opened on 21 November 1917 (O.S.) as the first parliament of autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> is said to have told a delegation that visited him to go home and organise. As Bolshevik detachments spread disorder, they occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chisinau">Chișinău</a> on 18 January 1918; members fled, some were arrested, and a secret session asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> for help. On 26 January the Romanian 11th Infantry Division under General Ernest Broșteanu entered Chișinău, and the pro-Soviet troops withdrew beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> after a battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Tighina</a>.</p><p>On 6 February 1918 the council voted unanimously for the independence of the Moldavian Democratic Republic, and a Ministry Council under Daniel Ciugureanu replaced the Directory Council. In the vote on union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, 86 members voted for it, 3 against, 36 abstained and 13 failed to appear; the vote did not divide clearly by ethnic group. The act of union listed special privileges for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, among them its own democratically elected diet and an agrarian reform, and King Ferdinand I sanctioned it by decree in Iași the same evening.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sfatul Țării” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A regional council, the <b>Sfatul Țării</b>, met in Chișinău and on 2 December 1917 proclaimed the Moldavian Democratic Republic as an autonomous member of a planned Russian federation, with Ion Inculeț as president.</blockquote><a href="/state/moldavian-democratic-republic">Moldavian Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
